Corrections to seventeen test cases of the W3C xslt30-test suite.

Five are separate mistakes. Twelve are one family, and are here because this
repository runs the suite's 2.0-applicable subset on a 2.0 processor as a
control, which the suite's own harness does not do: a test marked as applying
to a 2.0 processor that cannot be run by one shows up nowhere else.

1. package-021err and package-022err ask for XTSE3050 and cannot raise it,
   because neither stylesheet is well-formed XSLT. Both were edited on
   18 February 2020 for draft erratum E36, which requires a function named in an
   xsl:accept or an xsl:expose to carry its arity, and in both the "#0" landed in
   an attribute that does not take one:

     package-021err-used.xsl     on xsl:function's "name", which is an EQName
     package-022err-includeC.xsl on xsl:accept's "component", which is one of six
                                 words

   Neither spelling appears anywhere else in the suite. Their unedited twins,
   package-021 and package-022, were corrected again on 8 September 2020 and are
   right; the same erratum's other effects were withdrawn across decl/accept and
   decl/expose on 5 March 2023, under "remove unintended error, missing arity on
   function name". These two files were touched by neither pass.

2. accumulator-038 asks for XPTY0004 from evaluating an accumulator and cannot
   get that far. Its stylesheet is an xsl:package whose entry point,
   <xsl:template match="/" name="main">, declares no visibility and so is
   private (XSLT 3.0 3.5.3.1, "Otherwise, private"), while 2.3.4 requires the
   initial named template to be public or final on pain of XTDE0040. Twelve
   test-cases in the same test set were amended for exactly this - "Make main
   template public", eleven on 5 March 2019 and accumulator-031 as late as
   6 March 2023 - and this one was not. It is the only test case left in the
   suite that needs a private named template in an explicit package to serve
   as an entry point. Thirteen others name an entry template that carries no
   visibility attribute: twelve of them expect a static error, raised before
   the entry point is reached, and the thirteenth, forwards-011, makes its
   template public with an xsl:expose in the package manifest instead.

3. validation-0006 asks for XTTE1555 where the code is XTTE1540. It builds a
   parentless attribute with type="xs:integer" and a zero-length value, which
   is 25.4.1's "It is a type error if an [xsl:]type attribute is defined for a
   constructed element or attribute, and the outcome of schema validity
   assessment against that type is that the validity property ... is other than
   valid" - XTTE1540, whose own note in the specification gives
   <xsl:attribute name="dob" type="xs:date">1999-02-29</xsl:attribute> as the
   example. XTTE1555 is about something else entirely: "when validating a
   document node, document-level constraints (such as ID/IDREF constraints) are
   not satisfied". Nothing here validates a document node. Every other XTTE1555
   expectation in the suite is a duplicate ID, a dangling IDREF or a broken
   identity constraint.

4. validation-1702 asks for XTTE1510 where the code is XTTE1515. It runs
   validation-1701.xsl with the static parameter VALIDATION set to 'lax', and
   XTTE1510 begins "If the validation attribute ... has the effective value
   strict"; the lax code is XTTE1515. The test is validation-1701 with one
   parameter changed and the expected code left behind: 1701 (strict) asks for
   XTTE1510 and is right, 1703 and 1704 (preserve and strip) expect a result,
   and all four were created together on 21 December 2017.

5. Ten test cases are marked XSLT20+ and cannot be run by a 2.0 processor. In
   each, the stylesheet declares version="2.0", so it is not read
   forwards-compatibly, and then uses a construct XSLT 2.0 does not have, which
   a 2.0 processor is obliged to refuse. Each already passes on a 3.0
   processor. The marking becomes XSLT30+:

     sequence-0132         xsl:sequence with a sequence constructor
     collations-0128       xsl:evaluate
     collection-005        xsl:mode
     number-1004           xsl:iterate
     system-property-022   xslt:evaluate
     output-0724           html-version, and a named template xsl:initial-template
     output-0725           the same
     output-0726           the same
     result-document-0286  an EQName in the format attribute
     result-document-0287  the same

   Two more are marked XSLT20 - 2.0 only - and are genuinely 2.0 tests, each
   spoiled by one construct 2.0 does not have. Those are corrected in the
   stylesheet rather than in the marking, there being no later run to move them
   to:

   function-0302 writes element-available($zls || 'value-of'), and || is XPath
   3.0's. With concat() it is the same test and a 2.0 processor can read it.

   function-1902 exists to check that a 2.0 processor does not offer the XPath
   3.0 function library, and writes its diagnostic with
   <xsl:message expand-text="yes"> and text value templates, both of which are
   3.0's. The message says the same thing written with xsl:value-of.

Apply from the root of a clone of https://github.com/w3c/xslt30-test:

    git apply suite-corrections.patch

With this applied the xslt30-test 3.0 run here goes from 7,928 of 7,938 to
7,931, the 2.0 run from 5,616 of 5,639 to 5,617 of 5,629 - ten test cases
leaving a run they cannot be run in - and the schema-aware run from 8,526 of
8,540 to 8,531.

Not included: package-200, which asks for XTSE3000 where use-package-291 to 294
ask for XTSE0020 on the same shape - an unreadable package-version range on
xsl:use-package, with the named package available in both. That is a question
about which code the specification means rather than a typo, and is written up
in source/CodeDeeds.Xslt/Documentation/ConformanceNotes.md under "Which error an
unreadable version range is".

